  "explanation": [
    "Both filters are on expressions, so PostgreSQL guessed both: 0.5% for the equality on lower(country), 250 of 50,000 customers, and a third for the range on weight_kg * 1000, 3,333 of 10,000 products. The customers looked like the better start. It read all 50,000 and looked up their orders through orders_customer_id_idx, expecting 5,000.",
    "But IS and is are 30% of the customers, 15,000, and the lookups found 299,400 orders: 4 × (15,000 + 299,400) = 1,257,600. Reading the products, 10,000, and hashing the orders with the 10 that weigh 40 kg, 299,400 + 10, brings the plan to 1,916,726.",
    "Start from the other guess. Read the products, 10,000, look up the 10 heavy ones' 1,034 orders through orders_product_id_idx, 4 × (10 + 1,034) = 4,176, and their customers through customers_pkey, 4 × (1,034 + 1,034) = 8,272: the plan costs 23,798, for 316 rows. A guess knows the operator, not the data: an equality always looks rarer than a range, so PostgreSQL started from the wrong end."
  ]
